# XSH cfsetospeed()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The setting is made in a termios structure only; the first test uses a zeroed structure and needs
# no terminal, the second takes the attributes of a pseudo-terminal (XSI) and checks that the
# terminal is not touched. Rates are named by the Bxxx constants; a setter may refuse a rate (the
# page says it "may fail"), so only accepted rates are compared. Rates outside the defined
# set are not tested: the page leaves their handling open.

assertion  XSH.cfsetospeed.001
anchor     functions/cfsetospeed.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/cfsetospeed.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       cfsetospeed() with each of B0 B50 B75 B110 B134 B150 B200 B300 B600
           B1200 B1800 B2400 B4800 B9600 B19200 B38400 returns 0 or -1; when it
           returns 0, cfgetospeed() on the structure returns that rate; at least
           one call returns 0.
test       t/001-stores.c

assertion  XSH.cfsetospeed.002
anchor     functions/cfsetospeed.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       After cfsetospeed() on a copy of the attributes of a pseudo-terminal,
           with no tcsetattr(), a new tcgetattr() returns the output rate the
           terminal had before.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up that is
           not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the facility is absent.
test       t/002-no-hardware-effect.c
